INTRODUCTION 
 
Taking of the Population and Housing Census 2021 
The Census of Population and Housing is one of the important sources of statistical 
information for planning and policy making purposes. It entails a great deal of 
information relating to social, demographic and economic characteristics of the 
whole population.  
 
The census counted all persons who were present and been living in Samoa for at 
least three months on census night (November 6
th 2021) excluding Samoans  who 
were overseas for more than three months or visiting Samoa at the time for less than 
three months. The census was coordinated by the Samoa Bureau of Statistics.  
 
The Statistics Act 2015 
The conduct of the Population and Housing Census 2021, was conducted pursuant 
to Part II, Section 7 of the Statistics Act 2015 , whereby the Samoa Bureau of 
Statistics (SBS) is required to conduct the census of population and housing every 
five years, to meet national data needs of all stakeholders or to conduct the census 
at any other time as seen fit by the Minister of Statistics.  . 
 
Preparation and financial assistance 
 
The total cost of the PHC 2021 is amounted to ST1, 803,560.00.  The Government 
of Samoa funded the activity with financial assistance of around ST 100, 000    
from the UNPFA, which supported census publicity and awareness programs.  
  
Publicity and census awareness 
The publicity of the census 2021 theme “Everyone counts” or “E taua ma faitau 
tagata uma” in the Samoan language started in October 2020 up until the census 
enumeration was completed in the second week of November 2021. 
 
Listing and Mapping 
Before any census undertaking a listing of the households in all villages is  updated to 
ensure that all households will be covered during the census enumeration. The fieldwork 
took three months (August 2020 – October 2020) to cover more than 30,000 households. 
Twenty teams of eleven persons per team, were involved in the exercises.  
 
 
 7 
Questionnaire design and users’ consultation seminars 
 
In order to identify the user’s data needs , questionnaire consultation is always 
conducted. For the census 2021, the users consultation seminars were conducted for 
seven consecutive days (June 8th -16th 2021) in which all stakeholders from various 
sectors of the economy were invited to provide data needs to be collected during the 
census. At the end of the seven days consultations, all new questions were compiled 
for final selection by Samoa  Bureau of Statistics. In fact, not all the users’ data 
needs have been included in the final 2021 census questionnaire due mainly to the 
cost involved and limited time for census enumeration. Therefore, the final selection 
of questions was based on the linkage of the data being requested and the list of 
statistical indicators in the ‘Pathways for the Development of Samoa (PDS) 
‘2021/2022-2025/2026’ and the ‘Sustainable Development Goals’ (SDGs) 2030. 
All data requests outside of the two frameworks  were put aside to be integrated in 
other more appropriate survey activities by the bureau. 
 
The consistency of census questions with previous censuses and social and 
demographic definitions were kept for consistency and monitoring . To meet 
international standards, the International Classification of Occupations ( ISCO 08) 
and International Classification of Industries (ISIC Revision 4) were also used as 
frameworks for occupation and industry definitions respectively. This is also the 
first census in which the 4digit codes of the two frameworks were used in order to 
get the details breakdown of the occupation and the industries  in which the 
employed population are engaged to. 
 
 
Census pilot-testing and lessons learned 
 
The main purposes of the census pilot test were: to find out if the contents and flow 
of census questions were practical; to find out if the design layout was manageable 
for interviewing; to find out if the manual of instructions were sufficient to help the 
enumerators on the field; to find out if the training was effective and, lastly to find 
out how t he system  used for  data processing work s using the CAPI system. The 
pilot fieldwork was conducted at the village of Leauvaa on the 14th of October 2021. 
 
Organization of fieldwork and questionnaire quality checks 
The census date was November 6th, 2021.The enumeration started two  weeks 
before the census date in order to give enumerators sufficient  time to visit  and 
enumerate their allocated households. This is the first time temporary workers 
were hired as enumerators to conduct the census enumeration. They were 
recruited starting with the training for five days a week .  This follows our 
experience in using teachers and government employees in which some of them 
withdrawal in the last minute , which really  affect the fieldwork preparations. 
Another major challenge during this time is the national exam weeks always fall in 
the census enumeration period.  
 
 
 8 
A total of 220 temporary workers were recruited to help the SBS on the field work 
and the first to employ a person with disabilities, which supporter-enforces the 
theme for the 2021 Population and Housing Census. “Everyone counts”. 
 
For the census transportation, a total of 25 vehicles were used in the operation, 
with 8 vehicles provided by the Ministry of Finance from the pool of vehicles 
used for the Pacific Games , 6 vehicles from other Government Ministries namely 
Ministry of Women Community and Social Development, Ministry of the Prime 
Minister and Cabinet, Ministry for Revenue, Ministry of Justice, Courts and 
Administration, Ministry of Natural Resources and Environment  and the rest 
were hired vehicles.  
Challenges  
The sudden passing away of the former Government Statistician towards the final 
week of data collection really affected the  enumeration process , as staff was  
mourning the sudden event with so many emotions, in remembrance of her and 
her great contribution to the development of statistics in Samoa for many years.  
The spread of the COVID 19 to Samoa, that imposed lockdown and restrictions to 
travel even within Samoa further imposed delays in the census activities, 
especially with the call-backs. The State of Emergency restrictions  lead to the 
process of Data Integration, with face to face interviews put on hold.  
The commuting of people on everyday basis was the other major challenges not ed 
as most of the households had a vehicle for ease of travelling out of their home 
every time and this leads to the delay in data collection in some areas as the 
enumerators had to revisit those households for several times.   
However we believe that the setting up of district centers in each of the 51 districts 
will assist in hosting the enumerators within each district in the future censuses for 
ease of data collection activities and ease the challenge of travelling. 
 
Data Integration Process 
For the first ti me, Census 2021 dataset includes administrative (admin) 
information to impute records of people who were missed out during the census 
field operation. The Census 2021 dataset includes information obtained through 
field collection, as well as administrative  information, available to the Bureau 
through formal arrangements. Information are only added to the census dataset if 
they were in Samoa on census night, and have not  already responded to the 
census. Datasets includes  Census Listing 2020, Agriculture Census 2020 and 
Population Census 2016 , as well as the Civil registration and Health information 
registers. 
The household frame of more  than 30,000 households was used to collect and 
guide the enumerators during the fieldwork. This information was collected from 
the listing activity in 2020 in preparation for the census enumeration. The final 
Census 2021 dataset response rate was 98 percent.   
 
 9 
 
The use of administrative data has enabled us to information about people to 
include in the Census  2021 dataset where we are sure they should have been 
counted but unavailable during the enumeration period.  The focus is on the key 
census demographic information namely  age, sex, and geographic areas.  
In working with this process the Census will also look at data integration 
methodology in the near future in order to cater for unforeseen circumstances like 
pandemics while in the starting or middle of  data collection. We've also worked 
with our international colleagues at National Statistical Offices from New Zealand 
and Australia, leveraging of methods that they have developed, and their 
expertise, to provide input and review to our own work.  
 
Methodology  
Statistical Tools Used. 
In previous  Censuses, the data collection used paper form (PAPI) while data 
processing and  tabulation being done using CSPro 7 .5. Data was keyed in 
manually after the collection in the office. The PHC 2021 was the first in the 
series of censuses to adopt the Computer Assisted Personal Interview (CAPI) tool, 
a change that encourage efficiency and effectiveness in data collection, w ith 
assurance of quality.   
Analysis was performed using CSPro 7. 5 and excel application s. The 
questionnaire was administered via Computer Assisted Personal Interview (CAPI) 
using Android tablets and CSEntry CAPI software. The editing process was lead 
and administered by ACEO Data Processing and ACEO-Census divisions 
 
In order to have a final clean dataset to prepare indicators and different analysis, the Data 
Processing and Census division’s staff was working on the process of data editing and data 
cleaning. Once the raw dataset is cleaned, a clean copy of the dataset will be named as the 
‘latest edited data’ using dates of changes. By the 2nd week of October 2022, both the Census-
Survey and the Data P rocessing divisions managed  to complete all the edits on the census 
2021 raw dataset in preparation for the indicator in compilation process for the Fact sheet and 
selected tables prepared in the final report. 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
